{"parentUuid":"08e9d016-bc4f-4fdf-8e2b-1a527e5b9493","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"a954740a-3042-471d-8f4a-44e984522960","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"streamed-percolating-thunder","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013TV9djd6KwGQthTfPVzNAc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VZvWSRqPqMArC6L5w2oyBX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/413.lean"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3926,"cache_read_input_tokens":11453,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3926},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CYkjbpBV4DdLz3JHCYDqN","type":"assistant","uuid":"8d4b3bfb-b566-4aa2-81fb-77ffa18b28e8","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:38:26.464Z"}
{"parentUuid":"8d4b3bfb-b566-4aa2-81fb-77ffa18b28e8","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"a954740a-3042-471d-8f4a-44e984522960","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"streamed-percolating-thunder","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VZvWSRqPqMArC6L5w2oyBX","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import Mathlib.Data.Nat.Factors\n     2→import Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\n     3→import Mathlib.Data.Set.Finite.Basic\n     4→\n     5→open Set\n     6→\n     7→/-!\n     8→# Erdős Problem #413\n     9→\n    10→Let ω(n) count the number of distinct primes dividing n. A natural number n\n    11→is called a \"barrier\" for ω if m + ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n.\n    12→\n    13→Part 1: Are there infinitely many barriers for ω?\n    14→\n    15→Part 2: Does there exist an ε > 0 such that there are infinitely many n\n    16→where m + ε * ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n?\n    17→\n    18→Reference: [Er79, Er79d, ErGr80, Er92e, Er95c]\n    19→https://www.erdosproblems.com/413\n    20→-/\n    21→\n    22→/-- The number of distinct prime factors of n. -/\n    23→noncomputable def omega (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n    24→  (Nat.primeFactorsList n).toFinset.card\n    25→\n    26→/-- n is a barrier for ω: for all m < n, m + ω(m) ≤ n. -/\n    27→def IsBarrier (n : ℕ) : Prop :=\n    28→  ∀ m : ℕ, m < n → m + omega m ≤ n\n    29→\n    30→/--\n    31→Erdős Problem #413, Part 1 [Er79, Er79d, ErGr80]:\n    32→\n    33→There are infinitely many barriers for ω, i.e., infinitely many n such that\n    34→m + ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n.\n    35→-/\n    36→theorem erdos_problem_413_part1 :\n    37→    Set.Infinite {n : ℕ | IsBarrier n} :=\n    38→  sorry\n    39→\n    40→/--\n    41→Erdős Problem #413, Part 2 [Er79, ErGr80]:\n    42→\n    43→There exists an ε > 0 such that there are infinitely many n where\n    44→m + ε * ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n.\n    45→-/\n    46→theorem erdos_problem_413_part2 :\n    47→    ∃ ε : ℝ, ε > 0 ∧\n    48→    Set.Infinite {n : ℕ |\n    49→      ∀ m : ℕ, m < n →\n    50→        (m : ℝ) + ε * (omega m : ℝ) ≤ (n : ℝ)} :=\n    51→  sorry\n    52→"}]},"uuid":"803bc08a-ec17-451d-9680-60444dd50769","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:38:26.470Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/413.lean","content":"import Mathlib.Data.Nat.Factors\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Set.Finite.Basic\n\nopen Set\n\n/-!\n# Erdős Problem #413\n\nLet ω(n) count the number of distinct primes dividing n. A natural number n\nis called a \"barrier\" for ω if m + ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n.\n\nPart 1: Are there infinitely many barriers for ω?\n\nPart 2: Does there exist an ε > 0 such that there are infinitely many n\nwhere m + ε * ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n?\n\nReference: [Er79, Er79d, ErGr80, Er92e, Er95c]\nhttps://www.erdosproblems.com/413\n-/\n\n/-- The number of distinct prime factors of n. -/\nnoncomputable def omega (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n  (Nat.primeFactorsList n).toFinset.card\n\n/-- n is a barrier for ω: for all m < n, m + ω(m) ≤ n. -/\ndef IsBarrier (n : ℕ) : Prop :=\n  ∀ m : ℕ, m < n → m + omega m ≤ n\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #413, Part 1 [Er79, Er79d, ErGr80]:\n\nThere are infinitely many barriers for ω, i.e., infinitely many n such that\nm + ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n.\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_413_part1 :\n    Set.Infinite {n : ℕ | IsBarrier n} :=\n  sorry\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #413, Part 2 [Er79, ErGr80]:\n\nThere exists an ε > 0 such that there are infinitely many n where\nm + ε * ω(m) ≤ n for all m < n.\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_413_part2 :\n    ∃ ε : ℝ, ε > 0 ∧\n    Set.Infinite {n : ℕ |\n      ∀ m : ℕ, m < n →\n        (m : ℝ) + ε * (omega m : ℝ) ≤ (n : ℝ)} :=\n  sorry\n","numLines":52,"startLine":1,"totalLines":52}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8d4b3bfb-b566-4aa2-81fb-77ffa18b28e8"}
