{"parentUuid":"d7aa760f-f7c5-4bb5-afc2-be95c19afbda","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"a954740a-3042-471d-8f4a-44e984522960","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"streamed-percolating-thunder","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjJQ38D9XjGrSyqzWvo5KX","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import Mathlib.NumberTheory.Divisors\n     2→import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n     3→\n     4→open Finset\n     5→\n     6→/--\n     7→The sum of divisors function σ(n) = ∑_{d | n} d.\n     8→-/\n     9→noncomputable def sumDivisors (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n    10→  (Nat.divisors n).sum id\n    11→\n    12→/--\n    13→The k-th iterate of the sum of divisors function:\n    14→σ₀(n) = n, σₖ(n) = σ(σₖ₋₁(n)).\n    15→-/\n    16→noncomputable def iterSumDivisors : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ\n    17→  | 0, n => n\n    18→  | k + 1, n => sumDivisors (iterSumDivisors k n)\n    19→\n    20→/--\n    21→Erdős Problem #412 [Er79d, ErGr80]:\n    22→\n    23→Let σ₁(n) = σ(n), the sum of divisors function, and σₖ(n) = σ(σₖ₋₁(n)).\n    24→Is it true that, for every m, n ≥ 2, there exist some i, j such that\n    25→σᵢ(m) = σⱼ(n)?\n    26→\n    27→This conjecture, attributed to van Wijngaarden, asks whether the iterated\n    28→sum of divisors function eventually produces a single shared sequence for\n    29→all starting values ≥ 2.\n    30→-/\n    31→theorem erdos_problem_412 :\n    32→    ∀ m n : ℕ, 2 ≤ m → 2 ≤ n →\n    33→      ∃ i j : ℕ, iterSumDivisors i m = iterSumDivisors j n :=\n    34→  sorry\n    35→"}]},"uuid":"2825fb3a-6f08-49bb-a904-9c4fdefa1c38","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:38:26.708Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/412.lean","content":"import Mathlib.NumberTheory.Divisors\nimport Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n\nopen Finset\n\n/--\nThe sum of divisors function σ(n) = ∑_{d | n} d.\n-/\nnoncomputable def sumDivisors (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n  (Nat.divisors n).sum id\n\n/--\nThe k-th iterate of the sum of divisors function:\nσ₀(n) = n, σₖ(n) = σ(σₖ₋₁(n)).\n-/\nnoncomputable def iterSumDivisors : ℕ → ℕ → ℕ\n  | 0, n => n\n  | k + 1, n => sumDivisors (iterSumDivisors k n)\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #412 [Er79d, ErGr80]:\n\nLet σ₁(n) = σ(n), the sum of divisors function, and σₖ(n) = σ(σₖ₋₁(n)).\nIs it true that, for every m, n ≥ 2, there exist some i, j such that\nσᵢ(m) = σⱼ(n)?\n\nThis conjecture, attributed to van Wijngaarden, asks whether the iterated\nsum of divisors function eventually produces a single shared sequence for\nall starting values ≥ 2.\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_412 :\n    ∀ m n : ℕ, 2 ≤ m → 2 ≤ n →\n      ∃ i j : ℕ, iterSumDivisors i m = iterSumDivisors j n :=\n  sorry\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1,"totalLines":35}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d7aa760f-f7c5-4bb5-afc2-be95c19afbda"}
